---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import WifiQrCodeGenerator from '@/components/tools/generators/WifiQrCodeGenerator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="wifi-qr-code-generator">
  <WifiQrCodeGenerator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to make a Wi-Fi QR code</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Type the network name exactly as the router broadcasts it. SSIDs are case sensitive and a trailing space counts.</li>
      <li>Pick the security type. WPA/WPA2 covers almost every home and office router. Choose WPA3 only if the network runs WPA3-Personal, and “Open” only if there is no password.</li>
      <li>Enter the password. The code is built in this tab, and the password is not sent anywhere.</li>
      <li>Turn on “hidden network” only if the router hides its SSID.</li>
      <li>Download the PNG for a screen or the SVG for printing on a card.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Which devices can join from a Wi-Fi QR code</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Device</th>
            <th>Support</th>
            <th>How</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>iPhone and iPad, iOS 11+</td><td>Yes</td><td>Point the built-in Camera app at the code and tap the banner</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Android 10+</td><td>Yes</td><td>Camera app, or Settings → Network → Wi-Fi → Scan QR code</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Android 6–9</td><td>Usually</td><td>Needs a QR app that handles the WIFI: scheme</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Windows and macOS</td><td>No</td><td>No built-in support; join manually</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Smart TVs and consoles</td><td>No</td><td>Enter the password with the on-screen keyboard</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Android can also make a code for a network it is already joined to: Settings, Wi-Fi, tap the network,
      then Share. This tool works from the credentials, for a code you can print, laminate and leave on a
      table.
    </p>

    <h2>The WIFI: format</h2>
    <p>
      The payload is a plain-text URI that Google introduced with ZXing and that all major platforms now read.
      It looks like this:
    </p>
    <pre><code>WIFI:T:WPA;S:MyHomeWiFi;P:hunter2hunter2;H:false;;</code></pre>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Field</th>
            <th>Meaning</th>
            <th>Values</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>T</code></td><td>Security type</td><td><code>WPA</code>, <code>SAE</code> for WPA3, <code>WEP</code>, <code>nopass</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>S</code></td><td>Network name</td><td>The SSID, escaped</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>P</code></td><td>Password</td><td>Omitted entirely for open networks</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>H</code></td><td>Hidden SSID</td><td><code>true</code> or omitted</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Five characters are reserved and need a backslash before them inside a value: <code>\</code>,
      <code>;</code>, <code>,</code>, <code>:</code> and <code>"</code>. An SSID or password made only of
      hexadecimal digits is also ambiguous, because it could be read as raw hex, so it goes in double quotes.
      The Wi-Fi QR code generator applies both rules, and the panel under the code shows the exact payload.
    </p>

    <h2>Use a guest network</h2>
    <p>
      A printed QR code is a visible password. Anyone who photographs it, or sees it in the background of
      another person's photo, can join your network. That is acceptable for a guest SSID isolated from your
      other devices, and a bad idea for the network with your NAS and printer.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Use the guest network. Almost every router has one, and it keeps visitors off the subnet with your file shares and cameras.</li>
      <li>Turn on client isolation on the guest network so devices cannot see each other.</li>
      <li>Change the guest password from time to time and print a new card; it takes a minute.</li>
      <li>Do not put the code in the window. Inside, facing the seating area, is enough.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Troubleshooting a Wi-Fi QR code that does not connect</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Symptom</th>
            <th>Likely cause</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Camera reads the code but nothing happens</td><td>The app shows the raw text and does not act on it; use the system camera or the Wi-Fi settings scanner</td></tr>
          <tr><td>“Incorrect password”</td><td>A typo, or a curly quote pasted from a document in place of a straight apostrophe</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Connects then immediately drops</td><td>The security type is wrong: WPA3-only networks need SAE, not WPA</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Works on iPhone, fails on Android</td><td>The hidden-network flag is set on a network that is actually broadcasting</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Nothing scans at all</td><td>Not enough contrast, or the quiet zone was cropped when the image was placed in a document</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz bands often share one SSID. If your router gives them two names, the code joins
      only the one you typed. Guests with older devices can need the 2.4 GHz name.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
